382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Design Project: Camper Site Flooding Safety System)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Project Background )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Campsites located near rivers, lakes, or low-lying areas face flooding risks, especially during heavy rainfall. Flooding can damage camping equipment, disrupt activities, and endanger campers. To improve safety, innovative design solutions are needed that are practical, portable, and reliable in outdoor environments. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Project Task )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (The primary objective of this project is for you to design and develop a solution that enhances the safety of campers during flooding events. The system should either pre vent or mitigate flooding risks at campsites, using a combination of mechanical engi neering solutions, Internet of Things \(IoT\) technology, or a hybrid of both. The solution will need to be practical, cost-effective, and scalable for real-world use)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 241 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/A [1286 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 242 0 R
/T (Problem Statement)
/E (Problem Statement)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (The primary issue addressing this project is the critical absence of real-time, on-site monitoring systems at campsites. Current flood alert mechanisms operate on a macro scale, providing broad regional warnings that lack the granularity required for specific campsite safety. This lack of localized data creates a significant notification delay, often referred to as latency, between the onset of rising water levels and the reception of a warning. Consequently, campers are left with insufficient time to react, rendering them unable to evacuate before water levels become hazardous. This delay significantly increases the risk of entrapment, property damage, and casualty during flash flood events.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
